[{:seon.task/name "root-evals-through-the-one-repl"
  :seon.task/plan "Owner: \"agents ... connect to whatever repl server they need and for it to execute as faithfully as we can so they see what the seon agent sees.\" An MCP eval into an agent already goes through that agent's own REPL handler (middleware, the one printer, recorded :outside). An eval as root does not: seon.entrance/root-eval (entrance.clj:32-56) is a second REPL with plain read/eval/pr-str, intern of `sys`, no printer budget, no cost, no error map. Root's evals go through seon.repl/handler with a root session (sys in the session, *1 *e kept), printed by seon.print, recorded on main with origin :outside, like an agent's."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.entrance-test/a-root-eval-replies-like-an-agent-eval}}
 {:seon.task/name "inspect-a-kept-run"
  :seon.task/plan "Owner: \"Can we restore a session and replay repl commands and then an agent can debug a mostly faithful re-creation?\" Runs are kept (target/check/<time>/runs, next/runs, t5/out/*.store). A root opens a kept store for inspection: its reconciler observes and acts on nothing (no spawn, no kill, no judge) unless released, so a run's main and branches can be queried and its agents restored without resuming its work. bin/root --inspect STORE; the MCP tools work against it."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.entrance-test/an-inspected-run-starts-nothing}}
 {:seon.task/name "restore-and-replay-an-agent"
  :seon.task/plan "Owner: \"this ability to restore sessions and have agents go over the work with new instructions is huge. We can branch from any point right?\" Proved 2026-10-21 on t5/out/real3.store (a copy): Datahike keeps every commit as an immutable value with its parents (datahike.versioning/branch-history; branch! accepts a commit id; commit-as-db loads one); task-t5-edit-forms has 19 commits over 335 evals, and d/branch! from the middle commit made a branch holding exactly its 29 evals. So: branch from any commit at no copy cost, and for any eval between commits, branch from the commit before it and replay the rest. Restore: an agent forked on a kept branch at its end state (resume's load of the branch program). Replay to step N: an agent forked from the attempt's start (its basis), its recorded forms 1..N evaluated in order through its own REPL, every effect answered from its recorded outcome by a replay owner (nothing fires); each form's result compared with its record, a divergence recorded (nondeterminism, or a gap in the record). The debugging agent then works the re-creation through seon_eval, with new instructions as a task whose :evidence names the source run, branch and point. Needs every form's exact text (seon.repl/eval-record-keeps-a-failing-form-whole, widened to every form) and effect outcomes recorded whole (the model's reply text is on the turn; file and other effects' answers are not)."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/needs #{"seon.entrance/inspect-a-kept-run" "seon.repl/eval-record-keeps-a-failing-form-whole"}
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.entrance-test/a-replayed-agent-reproduces-its-recorded-results
                      seon.entrance-test/a-divergence-on-replay-is-recorded}}
 {:seon.task/name "reading-the-context-is-recorded"
  :seon.task/plan "Owner: \"how can we record this (don't code up a shitload of code around a harness, just make it so this is easy to do in whatever form we end up in)\". An outside agent working a task through the entrance (attach, context, eval, done) is already recorded by the system: the attempt on main, each eval on the agent's branch with origin :outside, the judgment at done. The one step missing is reading the context: seon.entrance's :context op returns the current-state block and records nothing. Record it as a turn on the agent's branch (origin :outside, the exact text returned), the same shape as a model turn, so an outside session reads, replays and mines exactly like a Seon agent's attempt. A few lines in the entrance; no harness."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.entrance-test/a-context-read-is-a-recorded-turn}} {:seon.task/name "the-context-op-answers-the-text"
  :seon.task/plan "Seen 2026-10-21 by lane AGENTS through the standing root (7888): printf '{:op :context :agent \"a1\"}' | nc answered {:context \"\\\"{:turn \\\\\\\"1 of 1\\\\\\\" ...\"}: the block printed twice, every quote escaped two levels deep, newlines as \\n, so an outside agent cannot read what the model reads. Cause: seon.entrance/answer :context (entrance.clj:92-93) takes (:printed (last items)) of submitting (seon.turn/context-block ...) to the agent, i.e. the REPL's printed form of a string value, which pr-str quotes; the entrance then prints the whole answer map again. Correct: the op answers the exact text the model would read (the string itself, once), e.g. by the agent answering the value's text rather than its printed form, or the entrance reading the printed string back once. Replaces nothing; deletes the double print."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.entrance-test/the-context-op-answers-the-models-text}}
 {:seon.task/name "attach-writes-nothing-when-it-is-refused"
  :seon.task/plan "Seen 2026-10-21 by lane AGENTS: {:op :attach :task \"seon.context/last-evals-fold-and-are-budgeted\"} on the standing root answered :seon.root/refused (branch held by live agent :a1), yet attach! (entrance.clj:62-71) had already written the task's env {:model \"outside\"} and :execution :running to main before spawn! refused. A refused attach leaves the task's desired state changed. Correct: spawn first (or check the branch's holder from main), and write env, release and the attempt in one transaction only once the agent exists; a refusal writes nothing.\n\nMeasured by lane tasks-at-once (2026-10-22): the same ordering races the reconciler, which spawns an outside-model attempt itself (`seon.reconcile/reconciler-proc`'s :started step handles it): the release commits, the reconciler forks its agent onto the task's branch, and attach's own spawn! is refused (\"branch :main/task-demo-t is held by live agent :a1\"). `seon.root-test/a-second-boot-resumes-the-same-main` read 0 attempts once under the full bin/test's load (green in two runs alone). One producer of an attempt's agent: the transaction above, written after the spawn, leaves the reconciler nothing to start."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.entrance-test/a-refused-attach-writes-nothing}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-door-defect-answers-that-request-alone"
  :seon.task/plan "From jolt/door (436f489, WIP). `seon.entrance/serve-connection!` answers a request whose verb throws, or whose line does not read, with an ad hoc `{:error :data}` (raw ex-data, which may not print readably). Correct: the typed one error map `:seon.entrance/defect` (`seon.contract/error`, declared in seon.problems.edn: the line it took, the cause's ex-data printed), answered to that request; the connection and every other connection (each its own edge thread) go on. The branch's `[connection n]` key is dropped: a connection is serial (one line in, one line out) and bin/mcp opens one connection per tool call, so the order already names the request. DRAGONS: its entrant-boundary row, and the half-open client row (a client that never sends or never reads parks only its own connection's thread)."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.entrance-test/a-door-defect-answers-that-request-alone}}
 {:seon.task/name "the-server-answers-mcp-itself"
  :seon.task/plan "Measured by root 2026-10-22: bin/mcp ran `exec ./seon mcp` (bin/mcp:11), booting the whole Seon binary (``seon.mcp/serve!``) only to relay JSON-RPC lines to root's server (seon.entrance): 729 MB physical footprint per process (725 MB dirty, the Chez heap with the program image; `footprint <pid>`), one per Claude session, 7 at once about 5 GB, past `:seon.config.machine/memory-mb` (4 GB) and uncounted by bin/check scale. v1's MCP server was the same: one JVM per client. The field's way: an MCP stdio server is newline-delimited JSON-RPC, so any byte pipe to a server that speaks it is the server (mcp-proxy, socat stdio<->tcp). So root's server speaks MCP itself, on its one port, framing told per line: a line opening `{\"` is JSON-RPC (`seon.entrance/mcp-reply`: initialize, tools/list from the one table `seon.entrance/ops`, tools/call through `seon.entrance/answer`, the same request/answer path; seon_context answers the block's text), every other line is line EDN as before. One port, because a second listener is a second accept loop and a second config key for the same verbs, and EDN and JSON-RPC lines never share a first two bytes. bin/mcp becomes a relay in stock perl (bin/root's holder already uses it): one connection per message, as ``seon.mcp/request!`` did, so a root restart (bin/root next) costs a session nothing; a message with an id that root does not answer (not listening, died mid-request) is answered the JSON-RPC error naming why, never a hang (seon.check/mcp-proof's root-down check, which replaces the deftest of ``seon.mcp/request!``'s unreachable text). Deletes `seon mcp`'s mode (check.clj), src/seon/mcp.clj and its DRAGONS row (the connect edge moves to bin/mcp, outside src)."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.entrance-test/the-server-speaks-mcp}}
]
